WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.429 --> 00:00:04.890
Welcome back to Curious Archive.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:10.760
This video is the finale of my two-part series
on the biology of the video game Subnautica.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:17.180
In these alien seas, breathtaking landscapes,
enormous predators, and unexpected mysteries

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.230
are all still waiting to be discovered.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:24.240
And just like how life in earth’s oceans
gets stranger the deeper you dive, the depths

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.369
of Planet 4546B are far more extreme than
what’s on the surface.

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:34.340
They’re also highly dangerous… and I can
confidently say that this ranks among the

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.600
scariest experiences I’ve had while doing
fieldwork for the Archive.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.510
But I’m determined to uncover the secrets
of this ocean.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:52.950
So, ride with me in my deep-sea submersible
as we conclude this expedition into the unknown…

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:59.730
To the north, beyond the grassy plateaus,
huge Tree Mushrooms grow in abundance.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.110
Despite their name and appearance, mushroom
trees are actually a particularly long-lived

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.210
species of coral.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:11.390
In the shade of these ancient giants, a new
classification of life is swimming.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:17.500
These are Jellyrays: an ethereal ray species
with highly translucent bodies.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.790
In Earth’s oceans, translucent lifeforms
are quite common in the lower-light depths.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:28.650
The jellyrays are a sign we’re getting closer
to the deeper — and more fantastical — regions

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:33.110
of 4546B.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:37.259
Beyond the safety of the Mushroom Forest,
the twilight of the Bulb Zone awaits.

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:42.760
Here, the luminescent Bulb Bushes dominate
the landscape, and have evolved to grow in

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:48.030
this rocky, sandy environment thanks to a
highly advanced system of roots.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:53.850
And along the sea floor, raging volcanic geysers
launch boiling liquid and chunks of molten

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.280
rock into the surrounding water.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:02.939
The extreme heat from these geysers charbroils
any fish that swims too close.

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:05.750
An ominous light flickers in the murk.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:11.980
The source is an ampeel: a huge, serpentine
predator covered with glowing prongs that

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.669
produce zaps of electricity.

00:02:14.669 --> 00:02:20.129
These aggressive creatures use electric discharges
to intimidate other predators, and to kill

00:02:20.129 --> 00:02:23.329
their prey with a single deadly shock.

00:02:23.329 --> 00:02:28.739
Such living batteries might seem biologically
inexplicable, but the curious electric eel

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:34.809
on earth might help demonstrate how a creature
could produce electricity.

00:02:34.809 --> 00:02:39.699
Electric eels use disc-shaped cells packed
into specialized organs to generate their

00:02:39.699 --> 00:02:43.279
electric currents — as incredible as that
might sound.

00:02:43.279 --> 00:02:49.000
It’s possible that the ampeel’s prongs
contain similar cells: although the bio-electricity

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.939
of the ampeel is far more extreme.

00:02:51.939 --> 00:02:58.290
A series of screeches announces the approach
of a legion of Bonesharks.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:04.529
Highly aggressive and territorial carnivores,
it’s unlucky to encounter so many at once.

00:03:04.529 --> 00:03:09.489
Their thickly-armored exoskeletons which give
them their names are adapted not just to deter

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:13.249
larger predators, but also other Bonesharks.

00:03:13.249 --> 00:03:18.249
The distinctive segmentation of these exoskeletons
suggests Bonesharks might share a distant

00:03:18.249 --> 00:03:21.629
relative to the Sand Sharks we glimpsed on
our last outing.

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:27.079
If so, it’s a fearsome family tree.

00:03:27.079 --> 00:03:31.449
While returning to the shallows to prepare
for the next leg of my journey, I spot a new

00:03:31.449 --> 00:03:33.790
type of colorful fish.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:39.181
Something about the way it looks is truly
hypnotic, and I want to swim closer, and closer…

00:03:39.181 --> 00:03:42.180
And then it bites my nose.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.879
That is a Mesmer, a sneaky little creature
that looks harmless while its four fins are

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:51.900
folded up, but unfurled can create hypnotic
patterns that have evolved to distract prey

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.219
while it goes in for the kill.

00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:59.360
Thankfully, it seems I’m a bit too big to
be swallowed up — but this is one fish to

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.680
avoid.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.819
With supplies replenished, it’s time to
venture further into the unknown.

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:12.999
Under the seafloor, networks of caves lead
deeper into the uncharted interior of planet

00:04:12.999 --> 00:04:15.099
4546B.

00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:20.299
Some of these caves are massive — and contain
entire ecosystems of their own.

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:23.759
And the Jellyshroom Cave is one of the most
miraculous.

00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:30.110
Here, among the stalagmites, grow Jellyshrooms:
massive, highly luminescent ¬flora which

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:35.370
might be distantly related to the tiny acid
mushrooms of the shallows.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:40.740
In the purple light of the Jellyshrooms shine
the Biters: small carnivores that stalk the

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.140
caves for small prey.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:48.370
The antenna on their forehead seems to resemble
the deep-sea anglerfish’s lure at first

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:54.180
glance, but the Biter’s antenna actually
serves an entirely different purpose.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.789
It contains olfactory organs that help it
detect bodily fluids in the water.

00:04:59.789 --> 00:05:03.800
In other words, the Biter can smell its prey
anywhere in the cavern.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:08.520
Unless, of course, the Biter becomes prey
itself.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:14.300
Winding through the cave is an unsettlingly
lengthy Crabsnake: the apex predator of this

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:15.449
environment.

00:05:15.449 --> 00:05:20.419
What makes this predator so deadly is not
just its size, but its ability to hide in

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:21.900
plain sight.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:26.889
The Crabsnake has a symbiotic relationship
with the local Jellyshrooms: using their hollow

00:05:26.889 --> 00:05:31.469
interior as the perfect lair from which to
ambush prey.

00:05:31.469 --> 00:05:36.060
In behavior and appearance, the Crabsnake
somewhat resembles Earth’s Bobbit Worm:

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:40.219
an unattractive predator that also ambushes
its prey.

00:05:40.219 --> 00:05:45.150
In the Jellyshroom caves, one needs to keep
an eye out…

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:49.860
Elsewhere on the seafloor, another shadowy
cavern beckons.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:55.150
After many hours navigating the cave system,
I surface in a cave that, strangely, seems

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:56.680
to be lit by… daylight?

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:05.300
I realize I’ve stumbled upon something exceedingly
rare for planet 4546B: dry land.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:10.280
This tiny island is the peak of an undersea
mountain connected to an aquatic cave system,

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:15.930
and it’s one of the few places on the planet
you can encounter terrestrial life.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:19.280
Scuttling on the beach are Cave Crawlers.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:24.940
As their name implies, these crab-like creatures
have evolved to crawl along the sides of cave

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:29.509
walls: which is likely why they are able to
adapt to this fleeting landmass relatively

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:31.229
quickly.

00:06:31.229 --> 00:06:36.300
Analysis of their biology suggests they possess
a gas exchange membrane to breathe air, instead

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.169
of fully-functional terrestrial lungs.

00:06:40.169 --> 00:06:45.930
Soaring overhead is a lifeform that has spent
much more time adapting to life.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:52.990
This is a Skyray: an avian creature that has,
remarkably, evolved powered flight.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:58.060
Although its wings give it some resemblance
to aquatic ray species, analysis shows no

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.349
particular genetic link.

00:07:00.349 --> 00:07:05.509
Instead, the Skyray appears to have split
off the evolutionary tree much earlier than

00:07:05.509 --> 00:07:11.979
most other species on the planet, perhaps
at a time when dry land was more prevalent…

00:07:11.979 --> 00:07:17.759
In any case, it seems we’ve now seen all
the island has to offer.

00:07:17.759 --> 00:07:20.050
Or… maybe not.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:24.060
On one end of the island is a tremendous artificial
spire.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:29.259
This mysterious monolith appears to be the
work of advanced extraterrestrials who, much

00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:32.150
like myself, came to this planet from afar.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:38.569
But did they come to study the life here,
or were their intentions more sinister…?

00:07:38.569 --> 00:07:45.389
In any case, it seems this planet’s greatest
secret is still waiting to be uncovered…

00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:49.969
To the south, the seafloor plunges lower than
ever before.

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:52.490
We have come upon the Grand Reef.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:57.889
Here, orb-like blue plants called anchor pods
risk floating off due to their gas-filled

00:07:57.889 --> 00:08:02.669
membrane — only staying in place due to
their net-like root system.

00:08:02.669 --> 00:08:08.009
And nearby stomp a herd of Sea Treader Leviathans.

00:08:08.009 --> 00:08:13.099
These vast herbivores roam the depths in search
of plants on which to graze.

00:08:13.099 --> 00:08:26.400
The thunderous nature of their rhythmic steps
is truly something to experience.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.550
Perhaps the most curious thing about these
creatures is the fact they have three legs

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:34.440
— something almost never glimpsed in nature.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:39.880
Analysis suggests their front leg is actually
an elongated snout, used to siphon plant material

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:43.089
from the sea floor, and to maintain balance.

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:48.250
As alien as this might seem, there are some
species of Earth Tripod fish with elongated

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:52.920
fins that they use similarly to stand on the
sea floor.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:09:03.000
Right now, it appears the Sea Treaders are
migrating, so it’s best not to disturb them…

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.950
Beneath the Grand Reef, caverns take us further
into the abyss.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:13.100
Here, the intimidating Crabsquid waits to
butcher prey with its ten limbs.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:18.230
Swim close to one, and they’ll unleash a
mysterious bio-electric discharge: which has

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:20.949
the properties of an electro-magnetic pulse.

00:09:20.949 --> 00:09:26.210
While they appear to have massive brains,
analysis suggests the large organ within their

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:32.170
translucent sac is actually their stomach,
which the Crabsquid fill with their bio-luminescent

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:33.170
prey.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:38.050
As a result, they seem attracted to light
sources, convincing me to make a hasty exit

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:39.899
from my submarine.

00:09:39.899 --> 00:09:44.870
This turns out to be a good call, as it drags
my submarine into the depths.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:49.589
If there are other creatures like the Crabsquid
on the prowl, I’m going to need something

00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:52.190
a little larger...

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:57.210
For the next perilous leg of the journey,
I’ll be piloting a ‘Cyclops’ submarine:

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:02.400
a vehicle that should be able to withstand
the crushing depth of the abyss.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:08.550
Below the deep Grand Reef awaits a surreal
environment of eerie green fog.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:14.490
This is the Lost River, a region covered in
alkaline brine pools, which give the illusion

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:16.830
of an underwater stream.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:21.760
Brine pools also exist in Earth’s oceans,
and are extremely toxic due to their high

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:22.889
salinity.

00:10:22.889 --> 00:10:25.740
Best not to get too close…

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:29.850
Above these pools drift the haunting Ghost
Rays.

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:34.070
Relatives of the Jellyrays from the Mushroom
Forest, in these extreme depths, the Ghost

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:38.930
Rays have become almost completely transparent,
to the point where you can see the red of

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:40.780
their internal organs.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:46.209
Elsewhere in the Lost River, Blood Crawlers
— the spindly relatives of the Cave Crawlers

00:10:46.209 --> 00:10:50.310
— stride above the brine on stilt-like legs.

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:54.639
Rather unsettling forms of life…

00:10:54.639 --> 00:10:58.250
In the central chamber, something truly horrifying
is waiting.

00:10:58.250 --> 00:10:59.250
Or rather, it would be if this leviathan were
still alive.

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:04.630
This ancient fossil belonged to a colossus
far larger than any that currently swims through

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:05.630
the seas.

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:12.149
Indeed, it is so massive that its ribs have
become part of the Lost River’s landscape.

00:11:12.149 --> 00:11:18.980
We should count ourselves lucky this unknown
genus seems to have gone extinct long ago…

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:22.279
Yet still alive are the River Prowlers.

00:11:22.279 --> 00:11:27.650
These aggressive, transparent predators haunt
the gravesite of the leviathan like vengeful

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:29.019
ghosts.

00:11:29.019 --> 00:11:34.230
Even more frightening, their four head-tendrils
can lash out when attacking, allowing them

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:36.570
to pull in prey.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:41.110
Not the sort of creature you’d like to encounter
in the dark.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:45.740
But there is a strange beauty to certain regions
of the Lost River.

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:51.810
In a cove under the central chamber, the brine
becomes a harmless blue, and Ghost Rays flock

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.320
around a massive tree.

00:11:54.320 --> 00:12:00.310
This tree appears to be an ancient nesting
ground, as it has grown around a giant bio-luminescent

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:01.310
egg.

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:08.209
While the egg is beautiful, whatever hatches
out of an egg that size might not be…

00:12:08.209 --> 00:12:13.230
To find the adult form of the huge creature
that hatches from this egg, we must venture

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:17.000
into the nightmarish darkness of the Dead
Zone.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:22.310
Here, at the edge of the habitable region
for most fauna lives, we’ve found our giants…

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:25.089
or they’ve found us.

00:12:25.089 --> 00:12:31.779
These aggressive monsters are Ghost Leviathans,
and they’re a major threat to this expedition.

00:12:31.779 --> 00:12:36.110
Analysis of their jaws suggest they feed on
microscopic lifeforms that help them reach

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:41.019
their massive sizes — although they also
seem fully capable of feeding on something

00:12:41.019 --> 00:12:42.019
larger.

00:12:42.019 --> 00:12:45.209
Time to head back the way we came…

00:12:45.209 --> 00:12:49.829
The mystery of the giant egg has been solved,
but that’s not the only strange thing in

00:12:49.829 --> 00:12:51.420
the Lost River.

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:56.329
These ruins of a massive alien structure resemble
the one on the island.

00:12:56.329 --> 00:13:02.019
The structure is guarded by these lifeforms,
the Warpers — who possess a mind-bending

00:13:02.019 --> 00:13:03.019
ability.

00:13:03.019 --> 00:13:08.079
At will, they can somehow fold spacetime and
vanish into portals.

00:13:08.079 --> 00:13:13.649
This is not the ability of a natural lifeform
—it seems we are dealing with an artificial

00:13:13.649 --> 00:13:15.380
creation.

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:17.910
Searching for answers, I enter the ruined
building.

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:22.819
In here, we can see the unfinished artificial
body of a Warper — confirming that they

00:13:22.819 --> 00:13:27.259
did not evolve naturally, and are a creation
of extraterrestrials.

00:13:27.259 --> 00:13:29.310
But for what purpose?

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:35.069
Translating a digital message, it seems these
alien architects came to 4546 B many years

00:13:35.069 --> 00:13:40.810
ago searching for a cure for a disease, and
the Warpers are their artificial quarantine

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.480
enforcers.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:49.480
Given the lack of any living architects on
4546 B, it appears that they were unsuccessful…On

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:55.010
a table in a nearby lab, they’ve left behind
the egg of a curious creature.

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:59.980
After returning to the surface to recharge
my submarine, I hatch the egg and this delightful

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:02.610
lifeform emerges.

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:08.759
This is a Cuddlefish: a friendly, rotund little
creature that displays high levels of intelligence

00:14:08.759 --> 00:14:10.139
and curiosity.

00:14:10.139 --> 00:14:14.440
I’ve also found it’s quite a fan of treats.

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:18.699
Analysis suggests it’s possible this creature
has been imported from another world — or

00:14:18.699 --> 00:14:21.699
even genetically altered to be extra friendly.

00:14:21.699 --> 00:14:27.410
It’s possible the architect aliens kept
them as pets just as we do with dogs.

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:33.170
It’s tempting to stay with the Cuddlefish
in the shallows, yet I can’t help but feel

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:37.950
that in the abyss, there are more mysteries
I’ve yet to solve…

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:43.410
Beneath the Lost River, the landscape blazes
with the fiery glow of molten rock.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:48.130
We’ve reached the Lava Zone: the deepest
environment of them all.

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:54.399
Here, incredible, heat resistant forms of
life have evolved: like the Crimson Ray — a

00:14:54.399 --> 00:14:59.100
ray species that has developed a mottled coloring
to help it blend in with the lava.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:05.610
And the Magmarang: a relative of the Boomerang
with a beautiful glowing tailfin.

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:10.839
Another remarkable form of life is the Lava
Larva, a grub-like creature that feeds off

00:15:10.839 --> 00:15:13.250
geothermal and electrical energy.

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:18.279
I first learned this fact when I found one
stuck to the windshield of my cyclops, draining

00:15:18.279 --> 00:15:19.279
its energy.

00:15:19.279 --> 00:15:24.069
While this behavior is somewhat annoying,
the energy-conversion capabilities of this

00:15:24.069 --> 00:15:28.350
creature are truly remarkable.

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:32.180
On the molten floor is something highly alarming.

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:37.270
These skeletons belong to Reaper Leviathans
like the one we encountered on our last expedition.

00:15:37.270 --> 00:15:38.270
Although picked clean, these skeletons belong
to recent kills.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:44.269
It seems that somewhere in the Lava Zone is
a predator so large it can drag Reaper Leviathans

00:15:44.269 --> 00:15:45.269
into the depths.

00:15:45.269 --> 00:15:48.819
This doesn’t bode well...

00:15:48.819 --> 00:15:53.310
Basking in the red glow of the molten rock
are Lava Lizards.

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:58.540
These predators are too small to be our culprits,
but they’re nonetheless quite deadly.

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:04.310
For defense, they are able to cover their
bodies with hardened lava like natural armor.

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:09.279
While such a behavior is far more extreme
than anything on Earth, Scaly Foot Snails

00:16:09.279 --> 00:16:13.670
— which I’ve discussed on this channel
before — can survive in volcanic vents by

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:17.800
incorporating iron scales into their body
like medieval armor.

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:22.579
However, Lava Lizard biology takes this to
a new level.

00:16:22.579 --> 00:16:25.720
At the end of the Lava Zone lurks a massive
chamber.

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:32.540
The loudest roar we’ve encountered on our
voyage shakes the cavern.

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:38.980
We’ve found our Reaper Leviathan slayer.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:45.040
This is a Sea Dragon Leviathan, the largest
predator in Subnautica.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:52.319
Possessing both arms and tentacles, these
creatures exceed 350 feet, or 112 meters in

00:16:52.319 --> 00:16:53.319
length.

00:16:53.319 --> 00:16:58.170
Sea Dragons get their name from their ability
to consume molten materials and expel them

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:01.519
at their adversaries like they’re breathing
fire.

00:17:01.519 --> 00:17:07.870
I can only hope this absurdly deadly lifeform
doesn’t notice me... no such luck.

00:17:07.870 --> 00:17:13.220
A huge blow from the leviathan rocks the cyclops
submarine.

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:16.400
As a result, there appears to be a… ‘minor’
leak.

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:19.890
Out of options, I have to abandon ship.

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:25.680
Luckily, I have one last hope in the Prawn
Suit: a deep sea mechanical walker that should

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:27.830
make an ideal escape pod.

00:17:27.830 --> 00:17:34.330
Ejecting from the cyclops, it’s a bit of
a drop to the bottom.

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:39.670
But it appears my plan has worked, as the
Sea Dragon Leviathan thinks its foe has been

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:40.970
vanquished.

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:46.340
Here, at the end of our journey, lies a final
mysterious building.

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:50.420
With some difficultly, I pilot the Prawn Suit
over to it.

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:58.430
Inside, locked in a containment facility,
the largest creature in Subnautica is waiting.

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:04.380
This is a Sea Emperor Leviathan, and although
it appears to be a relative of the Sea Dragon,

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:07.200
it is in no way hostile.

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:12.230
Like real-world baleen whales, the largest
creatures on earth, its diet consists solely

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:14.380
of microorganisms.

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:19.200
While the Sea Emperor Leviathan is so large
that I am unable to get a proper scan of it,

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:24.180
it seems that it can manipulate neurons to
communicate telepathically — as it told

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:25.540
me of its plight.

00:18:25.540 --> 00:18:30.180
The Sea Emperor was entombed here by the alien
architects in the hopes it could provide a

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:35.210
cure for their disease — which I believe
is in strict violation of the intergalactic

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:38.370
code of biological ethics.

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:43.040
At the request of this intelligent creature,
I agree to take its hatchlings back to the

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.040
surface with me.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:51.291
Watching the first moments of baby Sea Emperors’
lives is a truly wonderous experience: even

00:18:51.291 --> 00:18:57.330
after all the biological marvels I’ve glimpsed
thus far.

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:04.490
Sadly, the Sea Emperor is at the end of its
lifespan, and informs me it won’t be coming

00:19:04.490 --> 00:19:05.680
with us.

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:09.690
At the very least, I’m happy I can help
their babies live on in freedom.

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:16.180
It is a bittersweet conclusion to a long and
winding journey…

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:20.960
With the Sea Emperor babies returning to their
natural habitat, our study of this region

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:23.290
of Subnautica is at its end.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:29.020
There are other worlds waiting for us, and
the time has come to return to the stars.

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:31.090
Thanks for watching.

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:35.960
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:39.380
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:40.700
See you in the next video.

